 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Same flavours as the <a href=\"cucumber-sunomono.html\">cucumber pickle</a>, scaled up to cover:<ul class=\"ing two-col\"><li><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"150\" data-s=\"0\">150 ml</span> vinegar</li><li><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"75\" data-s=\"0\">75 ml</span> mirin</li><li><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"50\" data-s=\"0\">50 g</span> sugar</li><li><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"150\" data-s=\"0\">150 ml</span> water</li><li><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tsp\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 tsp</span> salt</li></ul></li><li>Sweeter and rounder; skip the kombu if you like it plainer.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Or use the cucumber marinade"
  },
  {
   "html": "<table class=\"mini\"><thead><tr><th>You have</th><th>Vinegar</th><th>+ Water</th><th>Sugar</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rice vinegar (~4.5%)</td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"150\" data-s=\"0\">150 ml</span></td><td>—</td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"60\" data-s=\"0\">60 g</span><span class=\"qalt\"> (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"5\" data-s=\"0\" data-alt=\"1\">5 tbsp</span>)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Korean brewed vinegar 양조식초 (~7%)</td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"95\" data-s=\"0\">95 ml</span></td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"3.5\" data-s=\"0\">3½ tbsp</span><span class=\"qalt\"> (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"54\" data-s=\"0\" data-alt=\"1\">54 ml</span>)</span></td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"70\" data-s=\"0\">70 g</span><span class=\"qalt\"> (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"6\" data-s=\"0\" data-alt=\"1\">6 tbsp</span>)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Table vinegar 9%</td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"75\" data-s=\"0\">75 ml</span></td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"75\" data-s=\"0\">75 ml</span></td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"70\" data-s=\"0\">70 g</span><span class=\"qalt\"> (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"6\" data-s=\"0\" data-alt=\"1\">6 tbsp</span>)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Vinegar 6%</td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"110\" data-s=\"0\">110 ml</span></td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"2.5\" data-s=\"0\">2½ tbsp</span><span class=\"qalt\"> (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"38\" data-s=\"0\" data-alt=\"1\">38 ml</span>)</span></td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"70\" data-s=\"0\">70 g</span><span class=\"qalt\"> (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"6\" data-s=\"0\" data-alt=\"1\">6 tbsp</span>)</span></td></tr><tr><td>White / distilled vinegar 5%</td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"135\" data-s=\"0\">135 ml</span></td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 tbsp</span><span class=\"qalt\"> (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"ml\" data-a=\"15\" data-s=\"0\" data-alt=\"1\">15 ml</span>)</span></td><td><span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"70\" data-s=\"0\">70 g</span><span class=\"qalt\"> (<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tbsp\" data-a=\"6\" data-s=\"0\" data-alt=\"1\">6 tbsp</span>)</span></td></tr></tbody></table>Matched to rice vinegar’s ~4.5% acidity for the brine. Non-rice vinegars are sharper and less rounded, hence the extra sugar — taste and adjust.",
   "title_html": "Vinegar conversions (in place of 150 ml rice vinegar)"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li><b>양조식초</b> (brewed vinegar, ~6–7%) is the one the 7% row means.</li><li><b>2배식초 / 3배식초</b> are double / triple strength — use half / a third of the 7% amount and make up the rest with water.</li><li><b>빙초산</b> is glacial acetic acid (~99%): never use it as-is.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Reading Korean vinegar labels"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>These are starting points for a sweet-sour amazu brine — adjust the sugar to taste on the next batch.</p>",
   "title_html": "Amounts"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Just a hint of ginger — shave it thin with a vegetable peeler.</li><li>Kombu adds a little savoury depth.</li><li>Use both, or leave the kombu out.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Ginger or kombu?"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>The red skins colour the brine and the white flesh pink over a day or two — that’s normal.</p>",
   "title_html": "Pink brine"
  }
 ],
